A||||8||Larry C||"stiff" steering at low speeds 86 GT
Z||000000||Larry C||06-13-99||03:47 PM||||I just bought a 86 GT and at low speeds the steering seems "very stiff" and takes a lot of effort to turn. <br>The car will be used by my daughter & she loves it except this problem.  Front end checks out OK and mechanics don't have a clue right now.  Anyone have any experience with this type problem? or just part of the rack & pinion steering system?
Z||000001||Eric||06-13-99||04:15 PM||||That's funny, "low speed steering problem".  Are you aware that NO Fieros have power steering.  The whole point of the car is its tightness and feel.  If you take away the manual steering you lose feedback and end up with just another boring apathetic "sportscar".  You will get used to it, your daughter will get used to it, and, unless I'm not missing a major problem, the car will start to feel perfect with time.  Who are these mechanics?  
Z||000002||batboy||06-13-99||07:53 PM||||Manual steering does take more effort to turn at lower speeds than at higher speeds, but I've never thought that either of my two Fieros turned all that hard while in a parking lot, but a young lady who is used to power steering might think so. Do you have a friend or relative with another Fiero you might be able to compare it with just to make sure nothing is binding up? I used to own a 1965 3/4 ton Chevy truck with manual steering, talk about building up arm muscles! If nothing is out of order, you'll eventually get used to it, and yes, eventually love the positive feel of manual steering just like Eric says.
Z||000003||lowCG||06-14-99||02:44 AM||||Does the car have 215/60R14 tires? Might be adequate with some lesser size of tire if so,and help out with the effort.195/65 works,and is alot better in the wet too.<br>My power steering on another car began to squeal as the belt slipped after mounting slightly wider tires.
Z||000004||Racer 1||06-15-99||11:46 AM||||First of check all suspension joints up front and make sure there in good working condiition and greased.Then if you still have a problem step down in tire width in front.Finally if you still have problems turning the wheel get GEO metro 12" tires and rims and some how mount them on or sell the car to me!! lol
Z||000005||Bruce||06-15-99||03:51 PM||||My tires were the original Goodyear Eagle GT when I bought the car last April. They were cracked, even though the tread was good. I bought new BF Goodrich T/A and the ride and handling imroved immediately. 
Z||000006||lowCG||06-16-99||05:12 PM||||Good point racer,no need to make the rears any smaller! Just the front;forgot to mention that.Inspection is definately a good idea too.<br>Didn't these cars use a steering stabilizer?<br>Hydraulic cylinders can do wierd things sometimes,when they're old and have gotten air inside. Never seen this before,and would probably only do it in one direction only,but maybe?
Z||000007||Eric||06-16-99||09:04 PM||||Do the non 88s really need the stabilizers?  With new ball joints there is zero slop in the steering.  I used to think I needed one too, but now I wonder if there is a reason the other cars really need them in the first place.
Z||000008||lowCG||06-16-99||10:05 PM||||I removed mine,never had any problems.Must say that it's always a good idea to have your wheels and tires  RE-balanced after a week or so of use;the tires don't seat completely upon installation and fall out of balance, the fronts are way more noticeable.<br>Had a set trued once,was unbelieveably smooth at hi-speed.Usually not necessary for the street though.
